Scott asks that an effectively given domain be identified with a relation \(\mathrm{INCL}\) on the integers for which \(\mathrm{CONS}(n,m)\iff\exists k.\,\mathrm{INCL}(k,n)\land\mathrm{INCL}(k,m)\) and \(\mathrm{MEET}(n,m,k)\iff\forall j.\,(\mathrm{INCL}(j,k)\leftrightarrow\mathrm{INCL}(j,n)\land\mathrm{INCL}(j,m))\) are recursively decidable, and which is reflexive, transitive, has an index above every index, and gives some meet index whenever a pair is consistent; the hint system is \(\mathcal{D}=\{\{m\in\mathbf{N}\mid\mathrm{INCL}(m,n)\}\mid n\in\mathbf{N}\}\), and the question is whether that system is essentially any effectively given one. `CONS` and `MEET` are those two formulas. `InclStructure` also carries a primitive-recursive `meetIdx`, a greatest code `topIdx`, and primitive-recursive deciders `incl_dec`, `cons_dec`, and `meet_dec`; the axioms are the fields `incl_refl`, `incl_trans`, `topIdx_spec`, and `meetIdx_spec`, and `axiom_i` through `axiom_iv` only project them, with (iv) witnessed by `meetIdx` rather than by an unspecified search. `ofPresentation` sets `INCL n m` to inclusion of the enumerated neighbourhoods, copies `inter` and `masterIdx`, transports consistency across `subset_inter_iff`, and transports `MEET` across `meet_iff_interEq`. The forward half of that equivalence takes the meet condition at `j = k`, obtains \(X_k\subseteq X_n\cap X_m\), applies `inter_mem` and `surj`, and uses the meet condition at the enumerating index; the reverse half is `subset_inter_iff`. `toNbhd n` is `{m | INCL m n}`. `toNbhd_subset_iff` is \(S_n\subseteq S_k\iff\mathrm{INCL}(n,k)\): the forward direction instantiates the inclusion at `n` by reflexivity, and the reverse direction applies transitivity. `toNbhd_subset_inter_iff` splits containment in an intersection into the two inclusions, `toNbhd_inter_eq_iff` matches equality of an intersection with `MEET`, and `toNbhd_top` says the greatest code enumerates every index. `toSystem` takes those hint neighbourhoods, with master the full set of indices, and closes under intersection by reading a lower neighbourhood as a `CONS` witness and applying `meetIdx_spec`. `toPresentation` sends `MEET` and `CONS` back to relations 7.1(i) and 7.1(ii), and `toSystem_isEffectivelyGiven` is the resulting presentation. `ofPresentation_toPresentation_INCL` recovers `INCL` because inclusion of hint neighbourhoods is the inclusion of the enumerated neighbourhoods. `reconElem` sends an element to the filter of hint neighbourhoods of enumerated neighbourhoods it contains, proving the filter laws by `surj` on an intersection and by `toNbhd_subset_iff` for upward closure; `reconElemInv` collects the underlying neighbourhoods of the hint neighbourhoods in a reconstructed element. `reconIso` shows the two maps are inverse by `ofPresentation_toNbhd_eq_iff` and reflect the approximation order, and `reconstruct_isomorphic` is that order isomorphism, hence a domain isomorphism \(\mathtt{reconstruct}\,P\cong^{\mathsf{D}} V\). **Theorem:** partial — `ofPresentation`, `toSystem`, and `reconstruct_isomorphic` are the two directions and the hint, but `InclStructure` demands primitive-recursive deciders, including a decider for `INCL` itself, and a primitive-recursive meet function, where Scott asks only that `CONS` and `MEET` be recursively decidable and that a meet index exist. **Proof:** yes — inclusion of hint neighbourhoods is `INCL` by reflexivity and transitivity, their intersections are `MEET`, and the element correspondence is the filter of hint neighbourhoods of member neighbourhoods.
